    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Theme:</b> Where Is Your Book?</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Participate in choral reading sessions, supported by the teacher. reading_2: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures. phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can participate in choral reading with teacher guidance, recognizing familiar location words (e.g., 'under,' 'in,' 'on') in stories. Can match objects or characters in picture books by pointing when asked questions about location (e.g., 'Where is the cat?'). Can recognize the names of letters (e.g., K, L, M, N, O) during choral reading activities (e.g., pointing to 'O' in 'orange' and saying the name of the letter O during choral reading activities).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Where is...?" song or chant with TPR. For example, "Where is the book? On the desk! On the desk!" while pointing to classroom objects (book, pencil, chair, desk, bag, box). Students repeat chorally and use gestures/point to the actual objects, reinforcing previously learned vocabulary and prepositions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large picture card of a book. "This is a book." Then, places it "on" a toy desk. "The book is ON the desk." The teacher points to the word 'on' on a flashcard. The teacher then uses letter flashcards (K, L, M, N, O) and pronounces their names, asking students to repeat. The teacher shows a simple storybook and models pointing to words as they read, emphasizing prepositions and target letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks simple questions to check understanding. "Is the book UNDER the chair? (No.)" "Point to 'in'." The teacher displays a flashcard with 'in' written and asks students to point to it. The teacher also shows a picture of a book and a letter 'B' flashcard. "Is this a book? (Yes.)" "Is this letter 'K'? (No.)"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, brightly illustrated "big book" or projected digital storybook titled "Where is My Blue Pencil?". The story features simple, repetitive sentences like "The blue pencil is in the bag. Where is the bag? The bag is on the chair." It uses target vocabulary (pencil, bag, chair, desk, book, box) and prepositions (in, on, under, next to). The story also visibly highlights words with letters K, L, M, N, O (e.g., 'book' on the 'desk', 'marker' in the 'box').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Participating in choral reading sessions, supported by the teacher. Matching spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures. Recognizing familiar letters (K, L, M, N, O).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud clearly, pointing to each word as it is read, especially focusing on 'where' /w/ and 'book' /b/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and point to the pictures in the book as the teacher names objects and locations (e.g., "Point to the pencil! Point to the chair!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks simple "Where is the [object]?" questions (e.g., "Where is the bag?") and students respond by pointing to the correct location in the picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to words containing letters K, L, M, N, O in the story (e.g., 'book,' 'marker,' 'notebook') and asks, "What letter is this?" (e.g., pointing to 'M' in 'marker'), encouraging students to say the letter name or point to a matching letter flashcard.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Choral reading of simple texts, matching words to pictures. Recognizing letters K, L, M, N, O. Grammar: Prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to), WH-Questions for location. Vocabulary: chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil, marker, notebook.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3.1: Choral Reading and Pointing.</b> The teacher rereads the "Where is My Blue Pencil?" story, this time pausing for students to chorally repeat key phrases (e.g., "in the bag," "on the chair"). As students repeat, they point to the corresponding words in a simplified, larger-print version of the story (e.g., projected on a screen or a classroom poster). The teacher guides them by pointing to the words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3.2: Word and Picture Match.</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of classroom objects (book, pencil, bag, desk, chair) and separate word cards with their names, as well as prepositions (in, on, under). The teacher says a word (e.g., "book") and students find the word card and place it next to the corresponding picture. Then, the teacher says a preposition (e.g., "under") and students point to the word and then identify a picture showing "under" (e.g., a cat under a table).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3.3: Letter Hunt.</b> The teacher displays flashcards of letters K, L, M, N, O. Students then look at a printed page from the "Where is My Blue Pencil?" story and, with teacher guidance, circle or point to instances of these letters within familiar words (e.g., circling 'K' in 'bookcase' if present, 'L' in 'pencil,' 'M' in 'marker,' 'N' in 'notebook,' 'O' in 'on'). The teacher asks students to say the letter name when they find it.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [15-18 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Find the Object" Reading Game with Large Picture Mat. The teacher lays out a large floor mat depicting a classroom scene with various objects (desk, chair, shelf, bag, box) and spaces to place object cards. Students work in small groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes small object picture cards (book, pencil, marker, ruler) to each group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then shows a large sentence strip with a simple phrase like "The book is ON the desk." or "The pencil is UNDER the chair." The teacher reads the sentence aloud, pointing to each word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group must read/follow the sentence by taking their corresponding object card (e.g., 'book') and placing it correctly on the large floor mat according to the preposition (e.g., 'on the desk').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns reading a sentence strip with the teacher's help and placing the object. This integrates reading recognition with physical action, reinforcing location words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to point to the words "in," "on," "under" as they place the object.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [7-8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for participation, word recognition, and letter identification. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards with simple sentences (e.g., "book," "on," "desk") and asks students to point to the word matching what is heard (e.g., "Point to 'desk'!"). The teacher then shows a simple picture sentence (e.g., a book under a chair) and asks, "Where is the book?" Students respond by pointing to the correct preposition word ('under') from a choice of three (in, on, under) written on the board or individual cards. Students also practice recognizing and naming letters K, L, M, N, O on flashcards or within short words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in choral reading sessions (e.g., repeating phrases, pointing to words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to match spoken words (prepositions, object names) to their written form by pointing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of familiar letters (K, L, M, N, O) when prompted.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are shown two large picture cards: one with a happy face and "I can read words!" and another with a questioning face and "I need more help." Students point to the picture that best describes how they feel about finding words and letters in the story.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b> "Did you enjoy finding the words in our story today? (Thumbs up or down)." "Can you find the letter 'M' in this word 'marker'? (Students point to 'M')." </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of engaging students in reading and word recognition? (e.g., the big book, the 'Find the Object' game, letter hunt). Which prepositions or letters were most challenging for students to recognize? What adjustments are needed for the next reading activity or for students who struggled (e.g., more visual aids, more one-on-one guided reading, simpler texts)? How can I better support students in matching spoken words to written words?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and recognizing location words and letters. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students actively use these location words to describe where objects are in the classroom, fostering oral communication and interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>